== Whitehead Groups <sec:whitehead-groups>

In this section we give another definition of the Whitehead group
$K_1 lr((Lambda))$ of an arbitrary ring $Lambda$, and show that the new
definition agrees with that in @def:whitehead-determinantal-group. We then
obtain a localization sequence for $K_0$ and $K_1$ analogous to that in
@th:grothendieck-localization-sequence. The section concludes with a discussion
of elementary matrices and stability theorems.

=== Introduction <ss:whitehead-introduction>

Let $Lambda$ be an arbitrary ring. For each integer $n >= 1$, let
$GL_n lr((Lambda))$ be the group of all invertible $n times n$ matrices over
$Lambda$. We embed $GL_n lr((Lambda))$ in $GL_(n+1) lr((Lambda))$ by means of
the homomorphism
$ alpha -> mat(alpha, 0; 0, 1), quad alpha in GL_n lr((Lambda)). $
Now define the _general linear group_ $GL lr((Lambda))$ by
$
  GL lr((Lambda)) = union.big_(n=1)^infinity GL_n lr((Lambda))
  = limits(lim)_arrow.r GL_n lr((Lambda)).
$
<eq:stable-general-linear-group>
#idx("General linear group", target: [@eq:stable-general-linear-group])
Thus, each element of $GL lr((Lambda))$ is represented by a matrix
$alpha in GL_n lr((Lambda))$ for some $n$. If $beta in GL_m lr((Lambda))$ where
$m >= n$, then $alpha = beta$ in $GL lr((Lambda))$ if and only if
$beta = op("diag")(alpha, 1, dots, 1)$ in $GL_m lr((Lambda))$.

Now let
$ GL' lr((Lambda)) = [GL lr((Lambda)), GL lr((Lambda))] $
be the commutator subgroup of $GL lr((Lambda))$. We set
$ K_1 lr((Lambda)) = GL lr((Lambda)) slash GL' lr((Lambda)), $
<eq:whitehead-general-linear-abelianization>
an abelian multiplicative group called the _Whitehead group_ of $Lambda$ (see
the remark following @def:whitehead-determinantal-group).
#idx("Whitehead group", target: [@eq:whitehead-general-linear-abelianization])
#source(78)
Our first task is to establish an isomorphism between this $K_1 lr((Lambda))$
and the group defined in @def:whitehead-determinantal-group, which we shall
denote by $K_"det" lr((Lambda))$ for convenience.

Let us recall from §@sec:grothendieck-groups that $K_"det" lr((Lambda))$ is the
additive group generated by ordered pairs $[M, mu]$ with $M in cal(P)(Lambda)$,
$mu in Aut_Lambda M$, modulo the subgroup generated by all expressions
$
  [M, mu mu'] - [M, mu] - [M, mu']
  quad "for all" M in cal(P)(Lambda), quad mu, mu' in Aut M,
$
<eq:determinantal-composition-generators>
#idx("Determinantal K₁", target: [@eq:determinantal-composition-generators])
$ [M, mu] - [L, lambda] - [N, nu] $ <eq:determinantal-exact-generators>
for each ses
$ 0 -> (L, lambda) arrow.r^f (M, mu) arrow.r^g (N, nu) -> 0 $
<eq:determinantal-automorphism-sequence>
(see @eq:determinantal-exact-relation). We are now ready to prove

#theorem[
  There is an isomorphism $K_1 lr((Lambda)) tilde.equiv K_"det" lr((Lambda))$,
  which is induced by mapping each $T in GL_n lr((Lambda))$ onto
  $theta(T) = [F, phi]$, where $F = Lambda^((n))$ and where $phi in Aut F$ is
  determined by the matrix $T$.
] <th:whitehead-determinantal-isomorphism>
#nidx(
  "$K_\"det\" lr((A)) ≅ K_1 lr((A)) = GL(A) slash GL'(A)$",
  "Whitehead group",
  target: [@th:whitehead-determinantal-isomorphism],
)
#proof[
  We show first that $theta$ induces a homomorphism
  $K_1 lr((Lambda)) -> K_"det" lr((Lambda))$, also denoted by $theta$. To begin
  with,
  $
    theta lr((mat(T, 0; 0, 1))) = [F plus.o Lambda, phi plus.o 1]
    = [F, phi] + [Lambda, 1] = theta(T),
  $
  since $[Lambda, 1] = 0$ in $K_"det" lr((Lambda))$ by
  @eq:determinantal-composition-generators. Therefore $theta$ induces a map
  $GL lr((Lambda)) -> K_"det" lr((Lambda))$, which is a homomorphism by
  @eq:determinantal-composition-generators. Further,
  $theta(GL' lr((Lambda))) = 0$ since $K_"det" lr((Lambda))$ is abelian, so we
  now have a homomorphism $theta: K_1 lr((Lambda)) -> K_"det" lr((Lambda))$.

  We shall construct an inverse map $theta'$. To begin with, consider a pair
  $(M, mu)$ with $M in cal(P)(Lambda)$, $mu in Aut M$. Choose
  $N in cal(P)(Lambda)$ so that $M plus.o N = F$ is $Lambda$-free, and then
  $mu plus.o 1 in Aut F$. Relative to some $Lambda$-basis of $F$, this
  automorphism $mu plus.o 1$ determines a matrix $T in GL lr((Lambda))$, and we
  define
  $ theta'(M, mu) = "image of" T "in" K_1 lr((Lambda)). $
  Changing the basis of $F$ replaces $T$ by $U T U^(-1)$ for some
  $U in GL lr((Lambda))$, and thus has no effect on the image of $T$ in
  $K_1 lr((Lambda))$.

  #source(79)
  Suppose now that $M plus.o N' = F'$ is free, and form
  $mu plus.o 1' in Aut F'$, which determines an element
  $T' in K_1 lr((Lambda))$. In order to verify that $T = T'$ in
  $K_1 lr((Lambda))$, we consider the pairs
  $
    (M plus.o N plus.o M plus.o N', mu plus.o 1 plus.o 1 plus.o 1')
    quad "and" \
    (M plus.o N plus.o M plus.o N', 1 plus.o 1 plus.o mu plus.o 1').
  $
  In both pairs, the module which occurs is precisely $F plus.o F'$, and there
  is an isomorphism from one pair to the other, given by the map
  $(m, n, m', n') -> (m', n, m, n')$, interchanging the two copies of $M$. The
  first pair determines a matrix $mat(T, 0; 0, I)$, the second
  $mat(I, 0; 0, T')$, so now we have
  $ mat(T, 0; 0, I) = U mat(I, 0; 0, T') U^(-1) $
  for some invertible $U$. Therefore $mat(T, 0; 0, I)$ and $mat(I, 0; 0, T')$
  have the same image in $K_1 lr((Lambda))$, and consequently so do $T$ and
  $T'$. This shows that $theta'(M, mu)$ is well defined, independently of the
  choice of the complement of $M$.

  It is obvious that
  $
    theta'(M, mu_1 mu_2) = theta'(M, mu_1) theta'(M, mu_2),
    quad mu_i in Aut M,
  $
  so it remains to check the behavior of $theta'$ on an expression of the form
  @eq:determinantal-exact-generators. Consider an exact sequence
  @eq:determinantal-automorphism-sequence; complementing each of the modules so
  as to obtain a free module, we obtain a new exact sequence in which $L, M$,
  and $N$ are $Lambda$-free of finite rank, and we are trying to prove that
  $theta'$ annihilates the expression @eq:determinantal-exact-generators. Since
  the sequence $0 -> L -> M -> N -> 0$ is $Lambda$-split, we may write
  $M = plus.o.big_(i=1)^m Lambda e_i$, where $lr({e_1, dots, e_l})$ are a basis
  for $L$, and the images of $lr({e_(l+1), dots, e_m})$ are a basis for $N$. We
  now view $f$ as an embedding, and $g$ as a canonical surjection. The fact that
  $f$ and $g$ commute with the automorphisms of the free modules means that $mu$
  can be represented by a matrix of the form
  $ T = mat(T_1, U; 0, T_2), $
  where $T_1$ represents $lambda$, and $T_2$ represents $nu$. But it is easily
  verified that both
  $ mat(T_2, 0; 0, T_2^(-1)) quad "and" mat(I, ast; 0, I) $
  lie in $GL' lr((Lambda))$. Therefore mod $GL' lr((Lambda))$ we have
  (multiplicatively)
  $ T equiv mat(T_1 T_2, 0; 0, I) = T_1 T_2. $
  Thus $T = T_1 T_2$ in $K_1 lr((Lambda))$, that is
  $ theta'(M, mu) = theta'(L, lambda) dot theta'(N, nu) $
  for each ses @eq:determinantal-automorphism-sequence.
  #source(80)
  It thus follows that $theta'$ induces a well-defined homomorphism
  $K_"det" lr((Lambda)) -> K_1 lr((Lambda))$. Since each element of
  $K_"det" lr((Lambda))$ is of the form $[F, phi]$ with $F$ free, it is
  straightforward to prove that $theta$ and $theta'$ are inverses of one
  another, and the proof is complete.
]

