      determinantal version as well as that given by
      @eq:whitehead-general-linear-abelianization.],
    [Since $K_"det" lr((Lambda))$ is defined categorically (see Exercise
      @i:exc:categorical-module-properties), so also is $K_1 lr((Lambda))$. In
      particular, $K_1 lr((Lambda)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((Gamma))$ whenever the
      rings $Lambda$ and $Gamma$ are Morita equivalent. As a special case of
      this fact, an isomorphism $theta: Lambda tilde.equiv M_r lr((Gamma))$
      induces an isomorphism
      $theta_*: K_1 lr((Lambda)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((Gamma))$, defined by
      mapping each matrix $(x_(i j)) in GL_n lr((Lambda))$ onto the matrix
      $(theta(x_(i j))) in GL_(r n) lr((Gamma))$.],
    [Given any ring homomorphism $phi: Lambda -> Gamma$, there is a commutative
      diagram
      #determinantal-naturality
      where the vertical arrows are the isomorphisms obtained in Theorem
      @th:whitehead-determinantal-isomorphism. Here, $phi_*$ is induced by the
      map $GL lr((Lambda)) -> GL lr((Gamma))$, and
      $
        phi'[M, mu] = [Gamma times.o M, 1 times.o mu]
        quad forall M in cal(P)(Lambda), quad mu in Aut_Lambda M.
      $
      We leave the proof as an exercise for the reader.],
    [By @cor:regular-ring-k-g-isomorphisms,
      $K_1 lr((Lambda)) tilde.equiv G_1 lr((Lambda))$ for any left regular ring
      $Lambda$. More generally, let $Lambda$ be arbitrary, and let $cal(C)$ be
      the category of left $Lambda$-modules which admit a finite resolution by
      f.g. projective $Lambda$-modules. The same resolution argument as in
      @th:finite-homological-dimension-k-groups gives
      $K_1 lr((Lambda)) tilde.equiv K_"det" lr((cal(C)))$: the syzygies of these
